     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Dear R-users, </div><div><br></div><div>I want to create a marginal effect (ME) plot with a histogram overly with ggplot2 similar to what Berry et al recommends. See e.g. figure 4 in this article <a href=\"https://files.nyu.edu/mrg217/public/jop2.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://files.nyu.edu/mrg217/public/jop2.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370417828000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1i8-AZ7-0wv4cIKKqAfovC\">https://files.nyu.edu/mrg217/public/jop2.pdf</a>. </div><div><br></div><div>My main problem is that I am not getting the scale correct between the histogram and the ME-plot on the y-axis. The code below actually do produce some reasonable scales but many of the other models I have will also have proportional scales. This will mean that either the histogram will be very much squeezed or the ME-plot will be so. So my question is primarily how can one solve this issue by using two y-scales as Berry et al does where the left y-axis is scaled according to the ME plot and the right y-axis scaled according to the histogram as percentage of observations (or count). They use STATA to produce those figures by the way and therefore I cannot see any reason why R cannot do it. </div><div><br></div><div>My secondary question would be if one can use one y-scale but somehow weight or standardize the histogram scale according to the ME-scale in order to get the proportions correct. </div><div><br></div><div>Part of the problem is that I need to use two different dataframes for the ggplot() since I am using multilevel models. So the distribution of the histogram actually refers to the country level variable and therefore of a different length of the ME dataframe which is called df here. </div><div><br></div><div>All input is appreciated </div><div>Adel </div><div><br></div><div>The code and data follows here below: </div><div><br></div><div>#Plotting witht ggplot </div><div>library(ggplot2) </div><div>df&lt;-data.frame(moderator, ME=marginaleffect(ce,int,moderator), upperbound, lowerbound, zero=0) </div><div># country&lt;-data.frame(QoG=unique(test2$QoG)) #create country level data </div><div><br></div><div># plot ME-plot </div><div>p&lt;-ggplot(data=df, aes(x=moderator)) + geom_line(aes(y=ME)) + geom_line(aes(y=upperbound), linetype =2)  + geom_line(aes(y=lowerbound), linetype =2)   </div><div><br></div><div>#add titles </div><div>p&lt;-p+labs(title=&quot;DisFre-malnutrition&quot;)   + ylab(expression(&quot;ME&quot;*(Disaster~&quot;|&quot;~QoG)==partialdiff~deprivation / partialdiff~disaster)) + xlab(&quot;QoG \\n 90 % confidence interval&quot;) + geom_line(aes(y=zero), colour = &quot;black&quot;) </div><div><br></div><div>#add histogram </div><div>plot.Dis_malnut&lt;-p+geom_histogram(data=country, aes(x=unique(country$QoG)), binwidth=0.2, fill=&quot;grey&quot;, alpha = 0.5) </div><div><br></div><div>Two dataframes are being used (country and df): </div><div><br></div><div>&gt; country </div><div>            QoG </div><div>1  -0.477967769 </div><div>2  -1.135620117 </div><div>3  -0.733241379 </div><div>4  -0.744128227 </div><div>5  -0.250983655 </div><div>6  -0.521456242 </div><div>7  -0.799672484 </div><div>8  -0.055570535 </div><div>9  -1.324936271 </div><div>10 -0.848031044 </div><div>11 -0.887484789 </div><div>12 -1.055428028 </div><div>13 -0.099116251 </div><div>14 -1.352775812 </div><div>15 -1.773983240 </div><div>16 -0.420899957 </div><div>17 -0.401326120 </div><div>18 -0.760478318 </div><div>19 -0.623285472 </div><div>20 -0.610830665 </div><div>21 -0.193876818 </div><div>22 -0.527554154 </div><div>23 -1.128192663 </div><div>24 -0.246690780 </div><div>25 -1.344676495 </div><div>26 -0.089475445 </div><div>27 -0.421678632 </div><div>28 -1.695610404 </div><div>29  0.001595285 </div><div>30  0.146999508 </div><div>31 -0.673524976 </div><div>32 -0.664324105 </div><div>33 -0.792684734 </div><div>34 -0.248011217 </div><div>35 -0.506785154 </div><div>36 -0.583150029 </div><div>37 -0.669326663 </div><div>38 -0.467811704 </div><div>39 -0.127217963 </div><div>40 -0.376150042 </div><div>41  0.227377683 </div><div>42 -0.618546546 </div><div>43 -0.774520755 </div><div>44 -0.901198685 </div><div>45 -1.045684576 </div><div>46 -0.627113461 </div><div>47 -0.364053369 </div><div>48 -0.142448425 </div><div>49 -0.709951103 </div><div>50 -0.157085374 </div><div>51 -1.218622923 </div><div>52 -0.396265328 </div><div>53  0.728968382 </div><div>54 -1.088502169 </div><div>55 -1.245620608 </div><div>56 -0.220291585 </div><div>57 -0.678782761 </div><div>58 -1.082884192 </div><div>59  0.216494277 </div><div>60 -0.526152194 </div><div>61 -0.603548586 </div><div>62 -0.366379112 </div><div>63 -0.497926593 </div><div>64 -0.698222876 </div><div>65 -0.881126106 </div><div>66 -0.854197979 </div><div>67 -0.801141679 </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; df </div><div>    moderator          ME  upperbound   lowerbound zero </div><div>1       -1.80 -6.79503660 -1.86962766 -11.72044554    0 </div><div>2       -1.78 -6.63575681 -1.81158548 -11.45992813    0 </div><div>3       -1.76 -6.47647701 -1.75280888 -11.20014514    0 </div><div>4       -1.74 -6.31719722 -1.69324998 -10.94114446    0 </div><div>5       -1.72 -6.15791742 -1.63285704 -10.68297780    0 </div><div>6       -1.70 -5.99863763 -1.57157419 -10.42570107    0 </div><div>7       -1.68 -5.83935783 -1.50934100 -10.16937467    0 </div><div>8       -1.66 -5.68007804 -1.44609212  -9.91406396    0 </div><div>9       -1.64 -5.52079824 -1.38175686  -9.65983963    0 </div><div>10      -1.62 -5.36151845 -1.31625872  -9.40677818    0 </div><div>11      -1.60 -5.20223866 -1.24951493  -9.15496238    0 </div><div>12      -1.58 -5.04295886 -1.18143593  -8.90448179    0 </div><div>13      -1.56 -4.88367907 -1.11192488  -8.65543325    0 </div><div>14      -1.54 -4.72439927 -1.04087707  -8.40792148    0 </div><div>15      -1.52 -4.5